---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "COPR (Confirmation of Permanent Residence) in 2026: eCOPR, Validity, Landing and Your PR Card";
const description = "COPR and eCOPR explained for late 2026: when you get it, validity and expiry, landing in Canada, flagpoling limits, the PR card wait and using COPR in B.C.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/copr-confirmation-of-permanent-residence";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is a COPR in Canada?",
    a: "A Confirmation of Permanent Residence (COPR) is the document IRCC issues when your permanent residence application is approved. It shows your personal details, immigration category, destination province, any conditions, a Valid to date and the date you became a permanent resident. IRCC says it stays valid proof of status until you receive your PR card. If you're outside Canada it's the document you show at the border to complete landing.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between COPR and eCOPR?",
    a: "They record the same thing. People approved while outside Canada receive a COPR before they travel and present it at a port of entry. People approved while inside Canada confirm their status in IRCC's Permanent Residence Portal, and IRCC then uploads an electronic COPR (eCOPR) to their portal account. IRCC says the eCOPR can take up to a few weeks after you confirm you're in Canada. You print and sign it to use as proof of status.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long is a COPR valid?",
    a: "The Valid to date on the COPR is the last day you can land in Canada with it. IRCC ties that date to your travel document's expiry and your immigration medical exam, which is valid for 12 months. So a COPR usually expires on the earlier of those two dates. IRCC says it can't extend a COPR, so plan to travel before the Valid to date.",
  },
  {
    q: "What happens if my COPR expires before I land in Canada?",
    a: "You can't use an expired COPR to become a permanent resident, and IRCC says it can't extend one. Don't book travel on an expired COPR. Contact IRCC through your account or the contact details in your approval message and ask what it needs from you; this may include a new medical exam or a new passport. The outcome isn't guaranteed, so getting advice from a licensed representative is sensible.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I flagpole to activate my COPR?",
    a: "If you're already in Canada and approved, IRCC's standard process is online: you confirm through the Permanent Residence Portal and receive an eCOPR, with no trip to the border. The December 23, 2024 end of flagpoling covered work and study permits, not PR. CBSA has also limited flagpole services to set days and hours at 12 ports since May 2024, including five in B.C. If you were told to land at a port of entry, check CBSA's current hours first.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does the PR card take after landing?",
    a: "As of IRCC's November 12, 2026 processing-time update, the processing time for a first PR card for a new permanent resident was 30 days. You don't apply for the first card: in Canada you upload a photo in the portal, and at the border the officer confirms the Canadian mailing address it will go to. Times change, so check IRCC's tool.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I get a SIN and MSP with my COPR?",
    a: "Yes for the SIN: Service Canada accepts a COPR as a primary document for permanent residents if it is less than one year old, as well as the PR card. For MSP, B.C. permanent residents can enrol, but new residents normally wait the rest of the month they become B.C. residents plus two months, so newcomers from abroad should buy private insurance for that gap.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a },
  })),
};
