        <li><a href="#pr-card" class="hover:underline">Your PR card after landing</a></li>
        <li><a href="#using" class="hover:underline">Using your COPR before the PR card arrives</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        For most people the COPR is the first piece of paper that says, in plain terms, that the long wait is over. Whether you came through Express Entry, a provincial nomination such as the BC PNP, or family sponsorship, the approval ends in the same place: a Confirmation of Permanent Residence, followed by a short final step to actually become a permanent resident. That step is where small mistakes, such as letting the document expire or travelling at the wrong moment, can cause real trouble. This guide walks through it using IRCC's current pages, checked on November 16, 2026.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What a COPR Is and What It Shows</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC describes the Confirmation of Permanent Residence as proof that you've been approved for permanent residence, used to complete the final steps of becoming a permanent resident. Older paper versions carry the form numbers IMM 5292 or IMM 5688. IRCC says the COPR remains valid proof of your status until you receive your PR card, which is why it matters well after landing day.
      </p>
      <p>According to IRCC, the document shows:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>your personal information, such as name and date of birth (and, on the version mailed after Express Entry approval, your photograph)</li>
        <li>your immigration category</li>
        <li>the city and province of destination</li>
        <li>any conditions of stay</li>
        <li>a <strong>Valid to</strong> date and the expiry date of your travel document</li>
        <li>the date you became a permanent resident (completed at landing or when your eCOPR is issued)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A COPR is not the same thing as a PR card. The COPR is a one-time record of how and when you became a permanent resident. The PR card is the wallet card you use to prove status and, importantly, to board a plane, train, bus or boat back to Canada.
      </p>

      <h2 id="in-canada" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Getting Your eCOPR from Inside Canada</h2>

      <p>
        If you're physically in Canada when your application is approved, which is common for BC PNP nominees on work permits and Canadian Experience Class applicants, IRCC handles the final step online. Most people don't need an in-person interview. You don't have to do anything until IRCC contacts you by email (from an address ending in cic.gc.ca) or phone.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">The Permanent Residence Portal steps</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Watch for two emails.</strong> IRCC says the first asks for basic information and the second tells you your status is ready to confirm. Check your spam folder, and if you got duplicate first emails, reply to the most recent one.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the right account.</strong> If you applied through the Permanent Residence Portal, sign in with that same account. If you didn't, IRCC creates one and emails you a username and temporary password, which expires 30 days after the account is created. IRCC warns not to create your own account, because that can delay things.</li>
        <li><strong>Declare that you're in Canada.</strong> Under Category, choose "Confirmation of permanent residence" and confirm you're physically in Canada. IRCC notes that this declaration alone doesn't make you a permanent resident.</li>
        <li><strong>Receive your eCOPR.</strong> IRCC uploads the electronic COPR to your portal account. It says this can take up to a few weeks after you confirm.</li>
        <li><strong>Give a Canadian address and upload a photo</strong> for your first PR card. A P.O. box is accepted only in rural areas without home delivery, and IRCC won't mail the card to a third party such as a relative or representative.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Once the eCOPR arrives, print it and sign it. IRCC says you use the printed eCOPR to prove your status and apply for benefits, such as a social insurance number. A representative can help you, but IRCC says they can't sign in with your credentials or declare on your behalf that you're in Canada.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Don't leave Canada mid-process</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">IRCC can't confirm your PR status while you're outside Canada, and it asks you to tell it if you leave before status is granted. If you must travel before your eCOPR arrives, contact IRCC at the email address in your invitation message first. Also remember that returning by commercial carrier needs a valid PR card; if you leave after becoming a PR but before the card arrives, you'll need a permanent resident travel document to come back.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="outside" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Getting Your COPR from Outside Canada</h2>

      <p>
        If you're abroad when approved, IRCC issues your COPR before you travel. On its Express Entry approval page, IRCC says it will send you a COPR and, if you're from a country whose citizens need a visa to travel to Canada, a <strong>permanent resident visa</strong>. Visa-exempt travellers get the COPR alone. Your passport must be a regular, private citizen passport; IRCC doesn't accept diplomatic, government service or public affairs passports for immigration.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first thing to do is read it carefully. IRCC asks you to check that the information on the COPR matches your passport exactly. Then look at the Valid to date and plan your travel around it.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Situation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">What you receive</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">How you become a PR</th>
