            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Approved abroad, visa-exempt</td><td class="px-4 py-3">COPR</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Present it to a CBSA officer at a port of entry by the Valid to date</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Approved abroad, visa-required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">COPR plus permanent resident visa</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Present both at a port of entry by the Valid to date</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="validity" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">COPR Validity and Expiry</h2>

      <p>
        The Valid to date is the last day you can use the COPR to land. IRCC's COPR page says the expiry date reflects when your travel document expires, and IRCC has long tied COPR validity to both your passport and your immigration medical exam. IRCC's medical exam page states that results are valid for <strong>12 months</strong>. In practice, then, the COPR usually runs out on whichever comes first: your passport expiry or about a year after your medical.
      </p>
      <p>
        This catches people out because the medical clock starts when you take the exam, not when you're approved. If processing took most of a year, your landing window may be short. Check the date the day your COPR arrives.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">If your COPR expires before you land</h3>
      <p>
        IRCC is direct about this: "We can't extend your COPR, so you should make sure to travel to Canada before it expires." An expired COPR can't be used to become a permanent resident, so don't book flights on one. Contact IRCC through your online account, or the contact details in your approval correspondence, explain why you couldn't travel, and ask what it needs. If you don't come to Canada within the medical's validity, IRCC says you may need another exam, and a new passport may also be required. Whether IRCC will reissue the document depends on your file, so consider advice from a licensed immigration representative or lawyer if you're in this position.
      </p>

      <h2 id="landing" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Landing at a Port of Entry, and Flagpoling</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">What to bring and what happens</h3>
      <p>IRCC's current checklist for people arriving from abroad is short. Keep these with you, not in checked luggage:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>a valid passport or travel document</li>
        <li>your COPR, and your permanent resident visa if one was issued</li>
        <li>proof of funds to support yourself and your family, unless your program exempts you (check the proof-of-funds rules for your program on canada.ca)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        At the border, a CBSA officer checks your identity and documents, confirms you're arriving on or before the COPR's expiry date, and may ask questions to make sure you still meet the requirements. If you're admissible, you're admitted as a permanent resident and the officer confirms the Canadian mailing address for your PR card. If you're carrying CAN$10,000 or more, you must tell the officer; failing to declare it can lead to fines and seizure. And if your address changes within 180 days of arriving, IRCC says you must tell it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Can you still flagpole for PR in 2026?</h3>
      <p>
        "Flagpoling" means leaving Canada for the U.S. (or St. Pierre and Miquelon) and coming straight back to get an immigration service at the border. Three facts frame it in late 2026:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>The December 23, 2024 change was about permits.</strong> CBSA stopped issuing work and study permits to most flagpolers from that date, with limited exemptions. The announcement did not cover PR landing.</li>
        <li><strong>Services at busy ports are limited.</strong> Since May 27, 2024, CBSA has restricted flagpole services at 12 ports, including five in B.C.: Abbotsford-Huntingdon, Aldergrove, Boundary Bay, Douglas and Pacific Highway. The days and hours are posted on CBSA's website. A December 2024 regulation (SOR/2024-258) confirmed the minister can limit services, including processing of people seeking permanent resident status, to specific classes at specific ports.</li>
        <li><strong>For people in Canada, the portal is the standard route.</strong> IRCC's in-Canada process doesn't need a border trip, so for most inland approvals flagpoling isn't necessary and adds risk (U.S. entry rules apply when you cross).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If IRCC has specifically told you to complete landing at a port of entry, check CBSA's current flagpole hours for the crossing before you go, and carry the same documents as an arrival from abroad.
      </p>

      <h2 id="errors" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Fixing Errors on Your COPR</h2>

      <p>
        A misspelled name or wrong date of birth on a COPR can follow you into your PR card, SIN and, later, citizenship records, so fix it early.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Before landing:</strong> IRCC's Express Entry approval page says that if there's a mistake on your COPR, contact IRCC through your account. Do this as soon as you notice it, given the expiry clock.</li>
        <li><strong>On an eCOPR:</strong> IRCC's portal page says incorrect information needs an amendment request.</li>
        <li><strong>After landing:</strong> IRCC's Guide 5218 covers amendment requests (form IMM 1436, sent by mail). It corrects only errors made by immigration officials, for fields such as name, date of birth, place and country of birth, citizenship, sex or gender identifier and marital status. If approved, you receive a Verification of Status document free of charge, which you keep with your original COPR. Photo, height, eye colour, signature and immigration category can't be changed this way, and details that changed after you became a PR (for example, a name change after marriage) go to your provincial registrar instead.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Guide 5218 asks for photocopies, not originals, and warns that originals sent without a request won't be returned.
      </p>

      <h2 id="pr-card" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Your PR Card After Landing</h2>

      <p>
        You don't apply or pay separately for your first PR card. If you confirmed in Canada, IRCC starts production after your portal photo is approved. If you landed at a port of entry, the card goes to the mailing address the officer confirmed. As of IRCC's processing-time update of <strong>November 12, 2026</strong>, the processing time for a <strong>new PR card was 30 days</strong> (renewals and replacements for existing PRs showed 49 days). Times change monthly; see our <a href="/articles/ircc-processing-times-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">IRCC processing times guide</a> for the wider picture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plan trips with the card in mind. Without a valid PR card you'll need a permanent resident travel document to board a commercial flight back to Canada, so it's usually wise to wait for the card before leaving.
      </p>

      <h2 id="using" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Using Your COPR Before the PR Card Arrives</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Social insurance number</h3>
      <p>
        Service Canada lists a Confirmation of Permanent Residence as an accepted primary document for permanent residents, as long as it's <strong>less than one year old</strong>; a PR card also works. If you already have a SIN from a work or study permit, contact Service Canada to update your record with your new permanent resident status.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">MSP health coverage in B.C.</h3>
      <p>
        The B.C. government sets a wait period for new residents: the rest of the month you become a B.C. resident plus two more months, with the Ministry of Health deciding your residency date based on factors including immigration status. For newcomers landing from abroad, that typically means about three months without public coverage, so buy private insurance for the gap and apply for MSP as soon as you arrive. If you were already living in B.C. on a work permit with MSP, contact Health Insurance BC to update your status so your coverage continues under your new PR status. Waivers of the wait period are approved only in very serious and exceptional cases.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Other uses</h3>
      <p>
        IRCC says that if you're already in Canada, you use your COPR to apply for government services. Keep the original (or your signed eCOPR printout) somewhere safe and carry a copy. You'll need its landing date later, for example to count physical presence when you eventually apply for citizenship.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Right of Permanent Residence Fee</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC won't grant PR status until the right of permanent residence fee (RPRF) is paid. It rose from $575 to <strong>$600</strong> for applications received on or after April 30, 2026. Principal applicants and accompanying spouses or partners pay it; dependent children and protected persons are exempt. You can pay it with your application to avoid a delay at the end, and IRCC refunds it if you paid up front and the application isn't approved. If you didn't pay it earlier, IRCC will contact you with instructions before your COPR is issued.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-emerald-50 border-l-4 border-emerald-600 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-emerald-900 mb-2">COPR checklist</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-emerald-900">
          <li>Check every detail on the COPR against your passport the day it arrives.</li>
          <li>Note the Valid to date and book travel well before it.</li>
          <li>In Canada: don't create your own portal account, and don't leave the country mid-process.</li>
          <li>Carry the COPR, PR visa (if issued), passport and proof of funds in your hand luggage.</li>
          <li>Apply for or update your SIN and MSP right after becoming a PR.</li>
          <li>Wait for your PR card before travelling abroad, or get a travel document.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>
        Still at an earlier stage? See <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-after-nomination" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">what happens after a BC PNP nomination</a>, read <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how long it takes to get PR in Canada</a>, or check where you stand with the <a href="/" class="text-emerald-700 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>. If you're landing in the Lower Mainland, our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-move-to-vancouver-bc" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">moving to Vancouver</a> covers housing, transit and your first 90 days.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
